      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <windows>
The windows in your home are original and you have recently dedicated alot of time and effort to remove the window trims and air seal around them. During the blower door test, we confirmed that most of them are indeed well sealed, with perhaps the 2nd floor bedroom &amp; bathroom windows having the most "wear &amp; tear" in their casement cranks and weatherstripping. As there seem to be many houses along Miron Drive that are updating their 1990's double pane windows to new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows, I thought I would model a few windows for you - specifically I modeled an upgrade to the 6 x second floor windows. If you proceed with this upgrade, I recommend ENERGY STARÂ® full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K. For your house, I modeled a more efficient window with a U-Value no higher than 1.05 W/mÂ²K.

Savings:
By replacing the 6 x second floor windows in your house with ones that meets the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 153 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,950 in rebates (6 windows @ $325 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. Any additional eligible replacement windows can qualify for up to $325 each, up to the maximum $5,000 available through the Canada Greener Homes Program.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage</windows>
                <doors>
Just in case you may be thinking about upgrading any exterior entrance doors (including the one between the house &amp; adjoined garage), I though I would model an upgrade to the front entrance door to a new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified unit, If you replace this entrance door, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.

Replacing the door in the front of your house with one that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 7 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $162.50 in rebates (1 door units @ $162.50) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>
VENTILATION - HEAT RECOVERY VENTILATION (HRV)

Your house has an older Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit - a Venmar 5585 Compact. The HRV was unplugged while I was there and has a tool chest up against the opening (where the HRV core is cleaned and the unit can be serviced) - so I suspect this HRV has not been used in awhile ? 

Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient Heat Recovery Ventilation  (HRV) unit is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality. Based upon the high "occupancy" rate that you described for your home in the past &amp; present (combined with the computer software modeling I have performed) would indicate that your house does indeed rely upon an HRV to manage indoor air quality.

The current HRV (if it was being used) is not very efficient.  I suggest you research replacing the current HRV with an ENERGY STARÂ® HRV, with a minimum 75% heat recovery efficiency rating and includes low electricity use ECM fan motors. Ideally, this new replacement system would be designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.

Savings: By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency and has ECM fan motors (as compared to the efficiency of the existing HRV), is estimated to reduce your energy costs by $ 54 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.8 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Pellet Stove Heat for the Basement

With your furnace thermostat on the upper floor, you mentioned you often find it a bit cooler in the basement and you will frequently use the pellet stove in the basement. I can see the current Whitfield Optima pellet stove has been used alot and it is drawing its' combustion air from inside the house (whereas the furnace, hot water heater and wood stove insert (on the main floor) all draw their combustion air directly from the outside of the house.

As you use this basement pellet stove alot, perhaps this is the year that you may want to upgrade it to a new 80% efficient unit that has a dedicated &amp; sealed combustion air connected to the back of the pellet stove? The AEA doubled our rebates this year for eligible wood &amp; pellet stoves, so this may just be an ideal time for this upgrade.

Savings:
I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to use an 80% efficient, direct-vent pellet stove to supplement up to 75% of the heating for your basement. The calculated net savings (reduced propane gas cost for the furnace, less cost of wood pellets for the pellet stove) is about $660 per year. A qualified wood pellet stove is eligible for 50% of purchase cost up to $ 2,000 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

Even though the estimated annual savings are not huge you will still be using the wood pellet stove in the basement to provide supplemental heat to that area, making your time downstairs more comfortable &amp; enjoyable.
 
For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Renewable Energy Systems

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the rear (SE) facing roof, directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 3,919 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,276 and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:
https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 2 x bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.7 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace, hot water and wood stove are all direct vent appliances.  If you proceed to replace the wood pellet stove in the basement, please be sure it has its' own dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe installed and it is directly connected to the back of the stove.

During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in your home. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside
        - 2 x bathroom exhaust fans
        - Clothes dryer
        - An older HRV, however it was not plugged in or being used at the time of my evaluation)

If you choose to replace or install any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. For a new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Hay River

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:
Electricity - $17.77 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.35 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $1.32 per litre 
Propane - $0.82 per litre delivered, $3.78 per m3 piped to the house
Wood - $310.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $4.86 per bag, $258.57 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Contracted Senior Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333
Fax: (867) 873-0303
E-mail: k.baigent@aea.nt.ca
Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

